      "how_attributed": "scripts/curvature_identity_check.py recomputes _sym_curvature_freq on the cached E(Q) maps of every non-placeholder mode at the four ladder temperatures (the same evaluations counted here) and classifies each negative value: either _scha_branch's width solver found no downward sign change of its residual at one of the three stencil points (0, dQ, 2 dQ; dQ = 0.005 Ang) and returned its grid-nearest fallback width, or a self-consistent root existed at all three. Its totals equal the counts here and its two listed root-at-every-point cases match two of these condensed modes by (unit, T, harmonic frequency, curvature), so the remainder is the fallback class by count; that file does not list the fallback cases individually. Every negative curvature on a screened mode belongs to a condensed mode.",
      "what_the_code_says": "finite_t._sym_curvature_freq returns None when no bound Gaussian exists at Q0 = 0 ('not observed on the production set'); compute_finite_t_softmode then substitutes the signed effective frequency, which is negative for a condensed mode. The identity check skips None, so the equal totals mean no such substitution is among these values. scripts/curvature_identity_check.py's docstring states F''(0) = <V''>_{0,sigma} = M Omega^2 for a single even mode, i.e. the curvature is positive whenever a bound self-consistent Gaussian exists at Q0 = 0. For the root-at-every-point class it lists the fitted a, b, c 'so a fit artefact can be recognised: a > 0 means no small-Q instability'.",
